  <h1>India’s Food Security — PDS, Nutrition, and Emerging Challenges</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Food security remains a cornerstone of India’s welfare state. In 2024, Indian Express evaluated the Public Distribution System (PDS) and the National Food Security Act (NFSA, 2013), noting successes in ensuring basic grain availability but also highlighting issues of nutrition, leakages, and fiscal sustainability. For UPSC, this topic sits at the intersection of economy, social justice, and governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Coverage: NFSA covers ~81 crore people (~67% population).<br/>•	Grain Subsidy Bill (2023–24): ₹2.05 lakh crore.<br/>•	Foodgrain Stock: FCI holds ~70 MMT of rice and wheat.<br/>•	Nutrition Gaps: India ranked 111/125 in Global Hunger Index 2023.<br/>•	Malnutrition: 35% children stunted, 57% women anaemic (NFHS-5).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Hunger Mitigation: PDS ensured food access during COVID lockdowns.<br/>2.	Portability: One Nation, One Ration Card covers 90% of beneficiaries.<br/>3.	Stabilising Prices: PDS dampens food inflation.<br/>4.	Technology Integration: Aadhaar seeding, e-POS reduced ghost beneficiaries.<br/>5.	Global Model: India runs the world’s largest food subsidy program.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Leakages: Despite DBT and e-POS, diversion of grains persists (~10–15%).<br/>2.	Fiscal Stress: Food subsidy crowds out capital expenditure.<br/>3.	Nutrition Deficit: Focus on cereals, neglect of protein/micronutrients.<br/>4.	Climate Risks: Erratic monsoons affect procurement and supply stability.<br/>5.	State Variations: Tamil Nadu, Chhattisgarh more effective than UP, Bihar.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	NFSA (2013): Legal right to subsidised foodgrains.<br/>•	POSHAN Abhiyaan (2018): Focus on malnutrition and stunting.<br/>•	Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS): Supplementary nutrition for children/women.<br/>•	Food Fortification: Iron, folic acid, vitamin B12 in rice.<br/>•	PMGKAY (2020): Free foodgrains during COVID extended till 2024.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Social Justice: Protects poorest households from hunger.<br/>•	Economic Stability: Prevents rural distress, supports demand.<br/>•	Political Stability: Food security central to welfare politics.<br/>•	Human Capital: Nutrition linked to productivity and education outcomes.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance & Welfare): PDS, NFSA.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Agriculture): Food policy, procurement.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Food security is not just about hunger, but about dignity.”<br/>o	“India’s welfare state is built on the foundation of the PDS.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Political economy of welfare and rights.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Nutrition-Sensitive PDS: Diversify into pulses, oil, millets.<br/>2.	Reduce Leakages: GPS tracking, social audits, direct benefit transfers.<br/>3.	Climate-Resilient Procurement: Crop diversification in MSP procurement.<br/>4.	Fiscal Reforms: Balance food subsidy with capital investments.<br/>5.	Integrated Approach: Align PDS with Poshan Abhiyaan and mid-day meals.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s food security system has ensured grain availability but must evolve into a nutrition security system. Reforming PDS and integrating it with broader nutrition policies is key to tackling India’s malnutrition crisis. For UPSC aspirants, it highlights how welfare, economy, and governance interact.</p>
